    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#codrus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codrus.jpg" alt="Codrus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#codrus">Codrus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kodros-Schale.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karl August Baumeister (nach Braun), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ephesus lay on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> near the mouth of the Cayster River, on a bay that has since silted up and left its ruins several kilometres inland. The site was settled long before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greeks">Greeks</a> arrived: many scholars identify it with Apasa, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> kingdom of Arzawa named in Hittite texts, and Greek writers said that Carians and Leleges lived there first. According to the tradition recorded by Strabo and Pausanias, Ionian settlers led by Androclus, son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#codrus">Codrus of Athens</a>, founded the Greek city around 1000 BCE, after an oracle told them to settle where a fish and a wild boar would show them the place. The newcomers found a native cult of a mother goddess, whom they identified with Artemis. Her sanctuary in the marshy plain, the Artemision, received its first temple buildings in the eighth century BCE and became the centre of the city's identity. Early Ephesus was ruled by kings said to descend from Androclus, and the family kept the title of king and certain priestly honours long after monarchy ended. Ephesus was one of the twelve cities of the Ionian League, which met at the Panionion on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mount-mycale">Mount Mycale</a>, and by the seventh century BCE it was a substantial trading town.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#sardis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sardis.jpg" alt="Sardis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Bath-Gymnasium_complex_at_Sardis,_late_2nd_-_early_3rd_century_AD,_Sardis,_Turkey_(17098680002).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ephesus lived from its position at the western end of routes into the Anatolian interior, above all the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a>, capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a>. Grain, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a>, textiles and luxury goods passed through its harbour, and close ties with Lydia brought Lydian wealth and customs into the city. The Artemision dominated economic life. The goddess owned estates and fisheries, received dedications from kings and private donors, lent money and held deposits, so that the temple served as one of the great banks of the Greek world, while its right of asylum offered refuge to debtors and fugitives. A deposit beneath the temple contained some of the earliest known coins, small lumps of electrum from around 600 BCE, and the city's own coinage carried the bee, the emblem of the goddess. The cult was served by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eunuch">eunuch</a> high priest with the Persian title of Megabyzos and by colleges of priestesses and temple officials. Ephesus was also a home of Greek poetry and thought: the iambic poet Hipponax worked there in the sixth century BCE, the philosopher Heraclitus wrote his book of sayings around 500 BCE and reportedly deposited it in the temple, and the painter Parrhasius was born in the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#croesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/croesus.jpg" alt="Croesus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Croesus_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marco Prins, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ephesus was never a major military power, and its history is largely one of accommodation with stronger neighbours. In the seventh century BCE the Cimmerians raided western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, and later tradition said they threatened the Artemision. Around 560 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a> besieged the city; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#herodotus">Herodotus</a>, the Ephesians placed their town under the protection of Artemis by stretching a rope from the walls to the temple, some seven stades away, and then came to terms with him. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> defeated Croesus in 546 BCE, Persian generals reduced the Ionian cities one by one. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ionian-revolt">Ionian Revolt</a> the rebels assembled at Ephesus in 498 BCE before marching inland to burn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a>, but they were defeated near Ephesus on their return, and the city took little further part in the war. As a member of the Delian League, Ephesus paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#athens">Athens</a> rather than supplying warships. It went over to the Spartan side during the Ionian War (412–404 BCE), and Lysander made it his naval base, defeating the Athenian fleet at the Battle of Notium nearby in 406 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#agesilaus-ii">Agesilaus II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sparta">Sparta</a> gathered his army there for his campaigns of 396–394 BCE. In the Hellenistic wars its harbour and new walls made Ephesus a prize that repeatedly changed hands among the successors of Alexander.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Monarchy gave way early to rule by aristocratic clans, and in the sixth century BCE Ephesus passed through a series of tyrannies. The best known tyrant, Pindarus, was related to the Lydian royal house; when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a> took the city around 560 BCE he allowed Pindarus to withdraw and left Ephesus a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lydia">Lydia</a> with its institutions intact. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> the city paid tribute through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#satrapy">satrap</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sardis">Sardis</a> and was governed by local leaders acceptable to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persia">Persia</a>. Within the Delian League it seems to have had a more popular constitution, and its later political life swung between democratic and oligarchic factions backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#athens">Athens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sparta">Sparta</a> or Persia. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> arrived in 334 BCE he restored the democracy, recalled exiles and ordered that the tribute formerly paid to Persia go to Artemis instead. Hellenistic Ephesus was governed by a council (<em>boule</em>) and assembly (<em>demos</em>) that passed decrees, granted citizenship and honoured benefactors, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> created a council of elders, the <em>gerousia</em>. The Artemision was run by its own boards, such as the temple wardens (<em>neopoioi</em>), and its wealth, asylum and festivals gave it a political weight that every ruler of the city, Lydian, Persian, Macedonian or Roman, had to respect.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#plato"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/plato.jpg" alt="Plato" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#plato">Plato</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Marie-Lan Nguyen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Temple of Artemis at Ephesus was the city's greatest contribution to the ancient world. The archaic temple, begun in the mid-sixth century BCE by the architects Chersiphron and Metagenes with columns paid for by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#croesus">Croesus</a>, was among the first great marble temples of the Ionic order, and its fourth-century successor was counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. The cult of Ephesian Artemis, with its distinctive cult statue hung with rounded ornaments, spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>; Strabo records that Phocaean colonists took it to Massalia and its daughter cities. Heraclitus, with his teaching on the unity of opposites, constant change and the <em>logos</em>, became one of the most influential of the early Greek philosophers, read by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#plato">Plato</a>, the Stoics and modern thinkers. Ephesus' role as a meeting point of Greek, Lydian and Persian worlds made it a channel for ideas, art and goods between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Its later greatness as the leading city of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, a centre of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> linked with the apostle Paul and the Gospel of John, and a council city of the church in 431 CE, rested on the foundations laid in these centuries. The ruins are now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-corupedium"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-corupedium.jpg" alt="Battle of Corupedium" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-corupedium">Battle of Corupedium</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plain_of_Corupedium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jona Lendering, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The independent life of Ephesus ended gradually, through a combination of natural change and great-power politics. In 356 BCE a man named Herostratus burned the Artemision, traditionally on the night <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> was born; according to Strabo the Ephesians declined Alexander's later offer to pay for the rebuilding and raised a still grander temple themselves. The silt carried by the Cayster steadily filled the harbour and pushed the coast westwards. Around 290 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lysimachus">Lysimachus</a> moved the city to a new site between two hills closer to the sea, enclosed it with long walls and renamed it Arsinoeia after his wife, reportedly forcing reluctant citizens to move by blocking the drains of the old town. After his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-corupedium">Battle of Corupedium</a> in 281 BCE the old name returned, and Ephesus passed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> took it in 197–196 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE gave it to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pergamon">Pergamon</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#attalus-iii">Attalus III</a> died in 133 BCE and left his kingdom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, Ephesus sided with Rome against the pretender Aristonicus and became part of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, organised by 129 BCE, where it grew into the region's chief city.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Ephesus matters as the home of the Temple of Artemis, one of the Seven Wonders, and of the philosopher Heraclitus, and as a port where Greek, Lydian and Persian worlds met; it survived successive overlords to become the leading city of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>.</em></p>

